### What are `Optional.map`, `filter`, and `flatMap`?

`Optional` is a container that holds either one value or nothing. The three navigation methods let you transform and inspect that value without ever pulling it out and risking a null.

`map(fn)` applies `fn` to the contained value if present and wraps the plain result back into an `Optional`. `flatMap(fn)` is for when `fn` itself returns an `Optional`, so the result stays a single `Optional` instead of nesting. `filter(pred)` turns a present value into empty when it fails the predicate.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.util.Optional;

public class Account {
    private final long id;
    private final Branch branch; // may be null

    public Account(long id, Branch branch) {
        this.id = id;
        this.branch = branch;
    }

    public long getId() { return id; }
    public Optional<Branch> getBranch() { return Optional.ofNullable(branch); }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

public class Branch {
    private final String code;
    private final String manager; // may be null or blank

    public Branch(String code, String manager) {
        this.code = code;
        this.manager = manager;
    }

    public String getCode() { return code; }
    public String getManager() { return manager; }
}
```

### How do you navigate account to branch to manager safely?

The task is to extract the uppercase manager name from an `Optional<Account>`, returning a default if any link is missing. `getBranch` returns an `Optional<Branch>`, so that step uses `flatMap`. `getManager` returns a plain `String`, so that step uses `map`. A `filter` drops blank names, a final `map` uppercases, and `orElse` supplies the default.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Branch;
import java.util.Optional;

public class ManagerLookup {
    public String managerName(Optional<Account> account) {
        return account
                .flatMap(Account::getBranch)        // Optional<Branch>, so flatMap
                .map(Branch::getManager)            // plain String, so map
                .filter(m -> !m.isBlank())          // drop blank names
                .map(String::toUpperCase)           // transform in place
                .orElse("NO MANAGER");              // default when empty
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Branch;
import java.util.Optional;

public class ManagerLookupDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ManagerLookup lookup = new ManagerLookup();
        Account withManager = new Account(1, new Branch("MUM01", "Rajesh"));
        Account noBranch = new Account(2, null);
        Account blankManager = new Account(3, new Branch("DEL02", "  "));

        System.out.println(lookup.managerName(Optional.of(withManager)));
        System.out.println(lookup.managerName(Optional.of(noBranch)));
        System.out.println(lookup.managerName(Optional.of(blankManager)));
        System.out.println(lookup.managerName(Optional.empty()));
    }
}
```

```text
RAJESH
NO MANAGER
NO MANAGER
NO MANAGER
```

### Why does this avoid null checks?

Without Optional, the same navigation is a pyramid of null guards. Each dot could throw `NullPointerException`, so each needs its own check, and the default handling is scattered.

```java
// WRONG style: nested null checks, easy to miss one
public String managerNameNullChecks(Account account) {
    if (account != null) {
        Branch branch = account.getBranchOrNull();
        if (branch != null) {
            String manager = branch.getManager();
            if (manager != null && !manager.isBlank()) {
                return manager.toUpperCase();
            }
        }
    }
    return "NO MANAGER";
}
```

```java
// CORRECT style: one fluent chain, impossible to forget a check
public String managerName(Optional<Account> account) {
    return account
            .flatMap(Account::getBranch)
            .map(Branch::getManager)
            .filter(m -> m != null && !m.isBlank())
            .map(String::toUpperCase)
            .orElse("NO MANAGER");
}
```

### What is the difference between `map` and `flatMap` on Optional?

The choice depends only on what the step function returns. If it returns a plain value, use `map`, because `map` will wrap that plain value in an `Optional` for you. If it already returns an `Optional`, use `flatMap`, because `map` would produce a nested `Optional<Optional<X>>`.

| Aspect | `map(fn)` | `flatMap(fn)` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fn` returns | a plain value `X` | an `Optional<X>` |
| Result type | `Optional<X>` | `Optional<X>` |
| Wrapping behavior | wraps the result | flattens the result |
| Using the wrong one gives | `Optional<Optional<X>>` if `fn` returns Optional | compile error if `fn` returns a plain value |

```java
// fn returns Optional<Branch> -> use flatMap
Optional<Branch> branch = account.flatMap(Account::getBranch);

// WRONG: map on a function that returns Optional gives a nested Optional
Optional<Optional<Branch>> nested = account.map(Account::getBranch); // awkward, hard to chain

// fn returns plain String -> use map
Optional<String> manager = branch.map(Branch::getManager);
```

### How does the chain short-circuit?

The moment any step produces an empty Optional, every later step is skipped and the chain falls through to `orElse`. No later function runs, so nothing can throw on a missing link. This is what makes the chain equivalent to the guarded pyramid, but without the nesting.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Optional;

public class ShortCircuitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String result = Optional.<String>empty()
                .map(s -> { System.out.println("map ran"); return s.trim(); })
                .filter(s -> { System.out.println("filter ran"); return !s.isBlank(); })
                .orElse("DEFAULT");
        System.out.println(result);
    }
}
```

```text
DEFAULT
```

Neither the `map` nor the `filter` ran, because the Optional was already empty.

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is using `map` where the step returns an Optional, producing a nested Optional that is clumsy to unwrap.

```java
// WRONG: double-wrapped, now you must unwrap twice
Optional<Optional<Branch>> nested = account.map(Account::getBranch);
Branch b = nested.flatMap(x -> x).orElse(null); // extra flattening needed
```

```java
// CORRECT: flatMap flattens in one step
Optional<Branch> branch = account.flatMap(Account::getBranch);
```

The second mistake is calling `Optional.get()` without checking, which throws `NoSuchElementException` on an empty Optional and defeats the purpose of using Optional at all.

```java
// WRONG: get on a possibly-empty Optional
String manager = account.flatMap(Account::getBranch)
        .map(Branch::getManager)
        .get(); // throws NoSuchElementException when empty
```

```java
// CORRECT: end the chain with orElse / orElseGet / orElseThrow
String manager = account.flatMap(Account::getBranch)
        .map(Branch::getManager)
        .orElse("NO MANAGER");
```

### What is the difference between `orElse` and `orElseGet`?

`orElse(value)` always evaluates its argument, even when the Optional is present, so it is wasteful if the default is expensive to build. `orElseGet(supplier)` defers to the supplier and calls it only when the Optional is empty.

```java
// orElse always builds the default, even when not needed
String name = managerOpt.orElse(expensiveLookup()); // expensiveLookup() ALWAYS runs

// orElseGet builds the default lazily, only when empty
String nameLazy = managerOpt.orElseGet(() -> expensiveLookup()); // runs only if empty
```

`orElseThrow` is the right ending when a missing value is a genuine error rather than a normal case.

```java
String manager = account.flatMap(Account::getBranch)
        .map(Branch::getManager)
        .filter(m -> !m.isBlank())
        .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalStateException("account has no branch manager"));
```

### What are edge cases and senior nuances?

A subtle edge case is a field that holds a plain nullable value rather than an Optional. Wrap it with `Optional.ofNullable` so `null` becomes an empty Optional instead of throwing. Never return `null` from a method declared to return `Optional`, because that reintroduces the very null you are trying to eliminate.

```java
// WRONG: returning null from an Optional-typed getter
public Optional<Branch> getBranch() { return branch == null ? null : Optional.of(branch); }

// CORRECT: ofNullable turns null into Optional.empty()
public Optional<Branch> getBranch() { return Optional.ofNullable(branch); }
```

Another senior point is not to use Optional for class fields or collection elements, because it adds boxing and does not serialize cleanly. Optional is designed for return types that may legitimately be absent. For a stream of accounts, `Optional::stream` (Java 9+) flattens present values neatly.

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

// Collect only the accounts that have a manager name
List<String> managers = accounts.stream()
        .map(a -> a.getBranch().map(Branch::getManager))
        .flatMap(Optional::stream) // drops the empties
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

### When should you use this chain?

Use the `flatMap`/`map`/`filter` chain whenever you walk nested data where any level may be absent and you want a default or a thrown error at the end. It is the cleanest alternative to nested null checks. Do not use Optional for simple flat values that are never null, where a plain field is clearer, and do not store Optional in fields or collections.
